[{:db/id 17592186087834 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-35dee2e4-b90f-575e-8987-9a600b4e60ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-35dee2e4-b90f-575e-8987-9a600b4e60ac" :question/id #uuid "35dee2e4-b90f-575e-8987-9a600b4e60ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-278400" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=2$ and $k=-1,$ so the center is located at $(2,-1).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=18$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=18,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=3\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}=6\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-72c3b159-addc-58ed-8523-aec1a6a17d47" "answer-field-344c48c4-4333-53a2-91c2-910da2275099"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-72c3b159-addc-58ed-8523-aec1a6a17d47" :answer-field/id #uuid "72c3b159-addc-58ed-8523-aec1a6a17d47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6350dda7-0009-5d3a-9b8a-8e8d61699c42" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6350dda7-0009-5d3a-9b8a-8e8d61699c42"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6350dda7-0009-5d3a-9b8a-8e8d61699c42" :answer/id 